    {
      "id": "BYtC",
      "code_hash": "cf91da4de8a1d98ff40b640962676d4a",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"local-explanations-of-the-binary-classifier\">Local explanations of the binary classifier</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">The local explanations take a binary classifier. Their result types have field names that fit every kind:</span>\n<ul>\n<li><code>Prediction.decision</code> is the predicted class, and <code>Prediction.output</code> is its probability.</li>\n<li><code>Prediction.score</code> is the sum of the scores on the scale of the link: the log-odds of the second class.</li>\n<li><code>Prediction.margin</code> is the log-odds of the predicted class. It is positive while the decision holds, so it means\n  the same thing for every decision. When the decision is <code>False</code>, the margin is the opposite of the score.</li>\n<li><code>Counterfactual.new_decision</code> is the class that the prediction changes to, and <code>new_output</code> is its probability.</li>\n<li><code>SufficientReason.minimum</code> tells whether no smaller set of features fixes the decision. It is always true for a\n  binary classifier.</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">The iris data has only 150 flowers, so the confidence intervals of the scores are wide. At a high confidence level,\nmost flowers have no counterfactual and no sufficient reason. Flower 70 is a versicolor near the border with the\nvirginica.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
